But the Federal Reserve began to

555
00:24:01,300 --> 00:24:03,800
see its role as not so much

556
00:24:03,800 --> 00:24:05,300
preventing banking crises, that

557
00:24:05,300 --> 00:24:06,800
was always in the background,

558
00:24:06,800 --> 00:24:09,800
but as stabilizing the economy.

559
00:24:09,800 --> 00:24:12,300
And so, the Fed began to

560
00:24:12,300 --> 00:24:14,800
think of itself as preventing the

561
00:24:14,800 --> 00:24:16,800
recurrence of recessions.

562
00:24:16,800 --> 00:24:22,800
So, when the economy was overheating, the inflation was

563
00:24:22,800 --> 00:24:24,800
building up, the Fed would

564
00:24:24,800 --> 00:24:26,800
raise interest rates, and the higher

565
00:24:26,800 --> 00:24:30,800
interest rates would cool down the economy.

566
00:24:30,800 --> 00:24:34,800
And when the economy got too soft, when the unemployment

567
00:24:34,800 --> 00:24:38,300
rate went up, the Fed would

568
00:24:38,300 --> 00:24:41,800
cut interest rates, and that would

569
00:24:41,800 --> 00:24:45,300
encourage borrowing, encourage spending,

570
00:24:45,300 --> 00:24:48,800
and boost the economy.

571
00:24:48,800 --> 00:24:51,800
Actually, that function of the

572
00:24:51,800 --> 00:24:54,800
Fed goes back even before 1933.

573
00:24:54,800 --> 00:24:57,300
And there was an economist,

574
00:24:57,300 --> 00:24:59,800
Charles Amos Dice, who wrote in

575
00:24:59,800 --> 00:25:06,800
the 1920s that the Federal Reserve is like the regulator

576
00:25:06,800 --> 00:25:08,800
on a steam engine.

577
00:25:08,800 --> 00:25:10,800
Do you know anything about steam engines?

578
00:25:10,800 --> 00:25:13,800
They have this thing that whirls around with two little

579
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:15,300
weights, and if the steam

580
00:25:15,300 --> 00:25:16,800
engine gets too fast, the weight

581
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:18,800
spins out by centrifugal force, and

582
00:25:18,800 --> 00:25:20,800
it cuts off the steam, so

583
00:25:20,800 --> 00:25:22,800
that it doesn't overheat.

584
00:25:22,800 --> 00:25:24,800
The engine doesn't get going too fast.

585
00:25:24,800 --> 00:25:28,800
And so, Dice said, the Federal Reserve is an invention.

586
00:25:28,800 --> 00:25:30,800
Now, that is serving a different, it's the regulator

587
00:25:30,800 --> 00:25:33,800
for the whole economy.

588
00:25:33,800 --> 00:25:35,800
And I think he was

589
00:25:35,800 --> 00:25:37,800
right, although it's not as accurate

590
00:25:37,800 --> 00:25:41,300
as a regulator on a

591
00:25:41,300 --> 00:25:44,800
steam engine, but it works somewhat

592
00:25:44,800 --> 00:25:45,800
well.

593
00:25:45,800 --> 00:25:47,800
I wanted to mention, every

594
00:25:47,800 --> 00:25:49,800
country now has a central bank,

595
00:25:49,800 --> 00:25:53,800
but I just wanted to mention the European Central Bank,

596
00:25:53,800 --> 00:26:04,800
because it's quite new, and it's maybe the biggest central

597
00:26:04,800 --> 00:26:07,800
bank now, in the sense that

598
00:26:07,800 --> 00:26:10,800
it, well, I shouldn't say that.

599
00:26:10,800 --> 00:26:14,800
It might be the biggest central bank by some standard.

600
00:26:14,800 --> 00:26:19,300
There was a treaty signed at

601
00:26:19,300 --> 00:26:23,800
Maastricht in 1992, which was a

602
00:26:23,800 --> 00:26:27,300
treaty that was signed by

603
00:26:27,300 --> 00:26:30,800
the European Union, which led to

604
00:26:30,800 --> 00:26:33,800
the creation of the European Union from the European

605
00:26:33,800 --> 00:26:37,800
Communities, and it also created a plan for a new

606
00:26:37,800 --> 00:26:44,800
currency called the Euro, which is a European currency.

607
00:26:44,800 --> 00:26:48,300
And the Euro did not

608
00:26:48,300 --> 00:26:51,800
actually start until 1999, and the

609
00:26:51,800 --> 00:26:52,800
currency, actual currency, was not issued until 1999.

610
00:26:52,800 --> 00:27:04,800
So, that is a relatively recent invention.

611
00:27:04,800 --> 00:27:12,800
The European Central Bank, or ECB, was founded in 1998.

612
00:27:12,800 --> 00:27:16,800
That's before the Euro currency started.

613
00:27:16,800 --> 00:27:18,800
They created a list of countries

614
00:27:18,800 --> 00:27:20,800
that wanted to participate in the

615
00:27:20,800 --> 00:27:24,800
Euro zone.

616
00:27:24,800 --> 00:27:29,800
The Euro zone, not all European Union countries decided to

617
00:27:29,800 --> 00:27:32,800
participate in the Euro.

618
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:34,300
Notably, the United Kingdom had

619
00:27:34,300 --> 00:27:35,800
a referendum and voted against

620
00:27:35,800 --> 00:27:37,800
it, and to this day, they

621
00:27:37,800 --> 00:27:39,800
are not members of the Euro.

622
00:27:39,800 --> 00:27:43,800
Also, Sweden, Lithuania, Latvia, Estonia, Poland, Czech

623
00:27:43,800 --> 00:27:48,800
Republic, Hungary, Romania, Bulgaria, and Malta are not in

624
00:27:48,800 --> 00:27:49,800
the Euro zone.

625
00:27:50,800 --> 00:27:52,800
Some of these countries that

626
00:27:52,800 --> 00:27:54,800
are not officially in the Euro

627
00:27:54,800 --> 00:27:57,800
zone use the Euro unofficially.

628
00:27:57,800 --> 00:28:00,800
It's not their currency, but there's no law against Euro

629
00:28:00,800 --> 00:28:02,800
coming in and spending Euros.

630
00:28:02,800 --> 00:28:07,800
So, the Euro seems more distributed than that.

631
00:28:07,800 --> 00:28:14,800
So, anyway, that's the most recent central bank.

632
00:28:14,800 --> 00:28:17,800
But it's the same general structure.

633
00:28:17,800 --> 00:28:18,800
Every European country has its own central bank.

634
00:28:18,800 --> 00:28:19,800
It's the same general structure.

635
00:28:19,800 --> 00:28:22,800
Every European country has its own central bank, like the

636
00:28:22,800 --> 00:28:26,800
Banca d'Italia or the Deutsche Bundesbank.

637
00:28:26,800 --> 00:28:28,300
But their original purpose is

638
00:28:28,300 --> 00:28:29,800
kind of gone, because they no

639
00:28:29,800 --> 00:28:30,800
longer maintain a currency.

640
00:28:30,800 --> 00:28:34,800
There are no more Deutschmarks or Italian Lira.

641
00:28:34,800 --> 00:28:37,800
They're all using the Euro.

642
00:28:37,800 --> 00:28:39,800
So, the real central bank

643
00:28:39,800 --> 00:28:41,800
is the European Central Bank in

644
00:28:41,800 --> 00:28:47,800
Frankfurt, led by Jean-Claude Trichet right now.

645
00:28:47,800 --> 00:28:48,800
So, that's the real central bank.

646
00:28:49,800 --> 00:28:59,800
Bank of Japan, by the way, became independent.

647
00:28:59,800 --> 00:29:01,800
I mentioned that in 1997.

648
00:29:01,800 --> 00:29:09,800
Bank of Japan, another very important central bank.

649
00:29:09,800 --> 00:29:11,300
There's been a movement in

650
00:29:11,300 --> 00:29:12,800
the last few decades to make

651
00:29:12,800 --> 00:29:14,800
central banks independent.

652
00:29:14,800 --> 00:29:16,800
This was something that our own Federal Reserve had from

653
00:29:16,800 --> 00:29:17,800
the beginning.

654
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:20,800
The Federal Reserve was designed to be separate from

655
00:29:20,800 --> 00:29:21,800
the government.

656
00:29:21,800 --> 00:29:24,800
The reason was, they thought that a government might want

657
00:29:24,800 --> 00:29:26,800
to inflate the currency.

658
00:29:26,800 --> 00:29:30,800
Political pressures might, at certain times, cause them to

659
00:29:30,800 --> 00:29:33,800
try to influence the central bank.

660
00:29:33,800 --> 00:29:36,800
And so, the Federal Reserve was

661
00:29:36,800 --> 00:29:39,800
set up so that the members

662
00:29:39,800 --> 00:29:42,800
of the Board of Governors had 14-year terms.

663
00:29:42,800 --> 00:29:46,800
They couldn't be kicked out, except for impeachment

664
00:29:46,800 --> 00:29:48,800
offenses.

665
00:29:48,800 --> 00:29:52,800
And so, the government couldn't control the central bank.

666
00:29:52,800 --> 00:29:55,800
The independent central bank has been very important.

667
00:29:55,800 --> 00:29:57,800
What tends to happen is, you

668
00:29:57,800 --> 00:29:59,800
bring people in who've had long

669
00:29:59,800 --> 00:30:03,800
careers in banking, who have a reputation for integrity, and

670
00:30:03,800 --> 00:30:05,300
you tell them that you

671
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:06,800
are the custodian of the currency.

672
00:30:06,800 --> 00:30:08,800
You bring in people who believe

673
00:30:08,800 --> 00:30:10,800
in the importance of a stable

674
00:30:10,800 --> 00:30:12,800
currency, and then you give

675
00:30:12,800 --> 00:30:14,800
them a 14-year term, and they're

676
00:30:14,800 --> 00:30:15,800
there.

677
00:30:15,800 --> 00:30:17,800
It's like the Supreme Court, almost.

678
00:30:17,800 --> 00:30:20,800
You can't kick them out.

679
00:30:20,800 --> 00:30:22,800
And some people think that's why

680
00:30:22,800 --> 00:30:24,800
the U.S. has had such a

681
00:30:24,800 --> 00:30:28,800
stable price level, because of our independent central bank.

682
00:30:28,800 --> 00:30:32,800
So many countries have fallen into inflation that has

683
00:30:32,800 --> 00:30:35,800
undermined the currency, but the U.S. hasn't.

684
00:30:35,800 --> 00:30:37,800
I think that's why there has

685
00:30:37,800 --> 00:30:39,800
been a move to copy the

686
00:30:39,800 --> 00:30:41,800
independent central bank.

687
00:30:45,800 --> 00:30:49,800
I wanted to talk now

688
00:30:49,800 --> 00:30:53,800
about specifics of what the central

689
00:30:53,800 --> 00:30:55,800
bank does.

690
00:30:55,800 --> 00:31:01,800
Notably, the Federal Reserve System has a committee called

691
00:31:01,800 --> 00:31:09,800
the Federal Open Markets Committee, FOMC, Federal Open

692
00:31:09,800 --> 00:31:12,800
Markets Committee, that meets around once a month.

693
00:31:12,800 --> 00:31:14,800
And they issue a statement.

694
00:31:14,800 --> 00:31:16,800
Every time they meet.

695
00:31:16,800 --> 00:31:20,300
And as it is now, actually,

696
00:31:20,300 --> 00:31:23,800
FOMC doesn't go back to 1913,

697
00:31:23,800 --> 00:31:25,800
but I'm talking about the

698
00:31:25,800 --> 00:31:27,800
Federal Reserve as it is now.

699
00:31:27,800 --> 00:31:33,800
This committee decides on a range for an interest rate

700
00:31:33,800 --> 00:31:35,800
called the Federal Funds Rate.

701
00:31:41,800 --> 00:31:44,800
And the Federal Funds Rate is an overarching interest rate.

702
00:31:44,800 --> 00:31:48,300
So, if you have an

703
00:31:48,300 --> 00:31:51,800
overnight interest rate that is charged

704
00:31:51,800 --> 00:31:54,300
on loans between banks

705
00:31:54,300 --> 00:31:56,800
and some other financial institutions,

706
00:31:56,800 --> 00:31:59,800
you generally would not have access to the Federal Funds

707
00:31:59,800 --> 00:32:00,800
Rate.

708
00:32:00,800 --> 00:32:02,300
Now, you probably don't need

709
00:32:02,300 --> 00:32:03,800
it, because you don't need an

710
00:32:03,800 --> 00:32:06,800
overnight loan anyway, right?

711
00:32:06,800 --> 00:32:08,300
Most of us would borrow

712
00:32:08,300 --> 00:32:09,800
money for more than one night.

713
00:32:09,800 --> 00:32:11,800
But banks, for various reasons,

714
00:32:11,800 --> 00:32:13,800
do this lending and borrowing

715
00:32:13,800 --> 00:32:17,800
in a day, at least under normal circumstances.

716
00:32:17,800 --> 00:32:21,800
So, we have an overnight interest rate.

717
00:32:21,800 --> 00:32:24,800
There's also longer federal funds, but we're emphasizing

718
00:32:24,800 --> 00:32:29,800
the overnight rate.

719
00:32:29,800 --> 00:32:31,800
And it's unsecured.

720
00:32:31,800 --> 00:32:34,800
This is just an unsecured, there's no collateral.

721
00:32:34,800 --> 00:32:37,800
It's an unsecured loan between banks.

722
00:32:37,800 --> 00:32:41,800
So, the interest rate reflects some risk, negligible risk,

723
00:32:41,800 --> 00:32:42,800
usually.

724
00:32:42,800 --> 00:32:45,800
And the interest rate is not unsecured, because banks trust

725
00:32:45,800 --> 00:32:47,800
each other, at least overnight.

726
00:32:47,800 --> 00:32:50,800
They know pretty much they're going to get paid back.

727
00:32:50,800 --> 00:32:53,300
And the current Federal Funds

728
00:32:53,300 --> 00:32:55,800
Rate in the United States is

729
00:32:55,800 --> 00:32:59,800
0.13%, as of last Friday.

730
00:32:59,800 --> 00:33:01,800
That's 13 basis points.

731
00:33:01,800 --> 00:33:05,800
So, it's virtually zero.

732
00:33:05,800 --> 00:33:08,800
This is a policy decision of the Federal Open Markets

733
00:33:08,800 --> 00:33:09,800
Committee.

734
00:33:09,800 --> 00:33:11,300
They have decided that the

735
00:33:11,300 --> 00:33:12,800
range for the Federal Funds Rate

736
00:33:12,800 --> 00:33:16,800
should be between 0 and 25 basis points.

737
00:33:16,800 --> 00:33:18,800
So, as of last Friday, it

738
00:33:18,800 --> 00:33:20,800
was exactly in the middle of their

739
00:33:20,800 --> 00:33:22,800
range.

740
00:33:22,800 --> 00:33:26,300
The FOMC uses its decisions to

741
00:33:26,300 --> 00:33:29,800
set the Federal Funds Rate as a

742
00:33:29,800 --> 00:33:31,800
way of stabilizing the economy.

743
00:33:31,800 --> 00:33:36,800
This is the regulator that Dice talked about.

744
00:33:36,800 --> 00:33:40,800
Right now, they've set it virtually at zero, because the

745
00:33:40,800 --> 00:33:42,800
economy is so weak.

746
00:33:42,800 --> 00:33:48,800
The unemployment rate last week was 8.8%, extremely high.

747
00:33:48,800 --> 00:33:51,800
And so, the Federal Reserve

748
00:33:51,800 --> 00:33:54,800
is not worried about inflation now.

749
00:33:54,800 --> 00:33:57,800
It's worried about high unemployment.

750
00:33:57,800 --> 00:34:00,800
And it's pushed it about as close

751
00:34:00,800 --> 00:34:03,800
to zero as it can get it.

752
00:34:03,800 --> 00:34:05,300
It can't go below zero,

753
00:34:05,300 --> 00:34:06,800
because you can't have negative interest

754
00:34:06,800 --> 00:34:07,800
rates.

755
00:34:07,800 --> 00:34:10,800
No one would lend at a negative interest rate.

756
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:12,800
So, that's where we are.

757
00:34:12,800 --> 00:34:15,300
Now, I wanted to just

758
00:34:15,300 --> 00:34:17,800
tell you about an interesting development

759
00:34:17,800 --> 00:34:25,800
that came in just in 2008.

760
00:34:25,800 --> 00:34:29,800
I mentioned that banks hold reserves.

761
00:34:29,800 --> 00:34:31,800
And the reason why banks

762
00:34:31,800 --> 00:34:33,800
hold reserves is because they have

763
00:34:33,800 --> 00:34:35,800
a very strong interest rate.

764
00:34:35,800 --> 00:34:37,300
And the reason why banks

765
00:34:37,300 --> 00:34:38,800
hold reserves is because they have

766
00:34:38,800 --> 00:34:39,800
a very strong interest rate.

767
00:34:39,800 --> 00:34:41,300
And the reason why banks

768
00:34:41,300 --> 00:34:42,800
hold reserve accounts at the Federal

769
00:34:42,800 --> 00:34:46,800
Reserve, traditionally, those accounts were not interest

770
00:34:46,800 --> 00:34:48,800
paying.

771
00:34:48,800 --> 00:34:51,300
I mean, banks had to either

772
00:34:51,300 --> 00:34:53,800
hold money or an account at the

773
00:34:53,800 --> 00:34:55,800
Federal Reserve for their reserves.

774
00:34:55,800 --> 00:34:57,800
And neither of them pay interest, right?

775
00:34:57,800 --> 00:34:59,800
If you hold money, you don't get any interest.

776
00:34:59,800 --> 00:35:01,300
If you actually have currency

777
00:35:01,300 --> 00:35:02,800
in your vault, you don't get

778
00:35:02,800 --> 00:35:03,800
interest.

779
00:35:03,800 --> 00:35:07,800
And until recently, banks didn't get interest on the

780
00:35:07,800 --> 00:35:08,800
interest rate.

781
00:35:08,800 --> 00:35:11,800
They didn't get interest on their deposits at the Federal

782
00:35:11,800 --> 00:35:12,800
Reserve.

783
00:35:12,800 --> 00:35:20,800
But that all changed in 2008 with the Emergency Economic

784
00:35:20,800 --> 00:35:24,800
Stabilization Act that President George Bush signed,

785
00:35:24,800 --> 00:35:26,800
ESA, as it's called.

786
00:35:26,800 --> 00:35:29,300
And ESA allowed the Federal

787
00:35:29,300 --> 00:35:31,800
Reserve to pay interest on the

788
00:35:31,800 --> 00:35:36,800
reserves held in the accounts at the Federal Reserve.

789
00:35:38,800 --> 00:35:41,800
So, the Federal Reserve has

790
00:35:41,800 --> 00:35:44,800
a policy now of paying interest

791
00:35:44,800 --> 00:35:46,800
on reserve balances.

792
00:35:46,800 --> 00:35:47,800
You see what I'm saying?

793
00:35:47,800 --> 00:35:49,300
I don't know what the Suffolk

794
00:35:49,300 --> 00:35:50,800
Bank did or the Bank of England

795
00:35:50,800 --> 00:35:52,300
did, but I know what

796
00:35:52,300 --> 00:35:53,800
the Federal Reserve is doing now.

797
00:35:53,800 --> 00:35:55,800
If a member bank puts

798
00:35:55,800 --> 00:35:57,800
money in deposit with the Federal

799
00:35:57,800 --> 00:35:59,800
Reserve, they will get an interest rate.

800
00:35:59,800 --> 00:36:01,300
And you can find out what

801
00:36:01,300 --> 00:36:02,800
the interest rate is by going to

802
00:36:02,800 --> 00:36:04,800
the Federal Reserve website.

803
00:36:04,800 --> 00:36:06,800
And right now, it is 0.25%.

804
00:36:06,800 --> 00:36:07,800
That's it.

805
00:36:10,800 --> 00:36:17,800
And so, that's an important policy change, because now it

806
00:36:17,800 --> 00:36:21,800
encourages the holding of reserves.

807
00:36:21,800 --> 00:36:27,800
Now, some people look at this and ask this question.

808
00:36:27,800 --> 00:36:30,800
Here's the Federal funds rate.

809
00:36:30,800 --> 00:36:37,800
Why isn't it the same as the interest on reserves?

810
00:36:37,800 --> 00:36:41,800
Interesting question.

811
00:36:41,800 --> 00:36:45,300
Why would any bank invest

812
00:36:45,300 --> 00:36:48,800
in the Federal funds market if

813
00:36:48,800 --> 00:36:50,300
they can get a higher interest

814
00:36:50,300 --> 00:36:51,800
rate by just holding a reserve

815
00:36:51,800 --> 00:36:53,800
at the Federal Reserve?

816
00:36:53,800 --> 00:36:55,300
There's been a lot of

817
00:36:55,300 --> 00:36:56,800
discussion of why that is now.

818
00:36:56,800 --> 00:37:00,800
It's a new phenomenon, because the interest on reserves goes

819
00:37:00,800 --> 00:37:04,800
back only less than three years.

820
00:37:04,800 --> 00:37:05,800
I think the simplest answer to

821
00:37:05,800 --> 00:37:06,800
the question is, why is it so?

822
00:37:06,800 --> 00:37:10,300
The first answer to the question

823
00:37:10,300 --> 00:37:13,800
is that member banks of the Federal

824
00:37:13,800 --> 00:37:17,800
Reserve System have stopped lending on the Federal funds

825
00:37:17,800 --> 00:37:19,800
market, basically.

826
00:37:19,800 --> 00:37:22,300
They just leave it in

827
00:37:22,300 --> 00:37:24,800
reserves, because that's a higher interest

828
00:37:24,800 --> 00:37:25,800
rate.

829
00:37:25,800 --> 00:37:27,800
So, who is lending at this?

830
00:37:27,800 --> 00:37:29,300
It turns out that there

831
00:37:29,300 --> 00:37:30,800
are some people, notably the government

832
00:37:30,800 --> 00:37:33,800
sponsored enterprises, like Fannie and Freddie, that are not

833
00:37:33,800 --> 00:37:35,800
eligible for interest on reserves.

834
00:37:35,800 --> 00:37:41,800
So, they have taken over the Federal funds market.

835
00:37:41,800 --> 00:37:44,300
The reason the Fed added interest

836
00:37:44,300 --> 00:37:46,800
on reserves is to create another

837
00:37:46,800 --> 00:37:48,800
tool of monetary policy.

838
00:37:48,800 --> 00:37:51,300
There's a lot of concern that after

839
00:37:51,300 --> 00:37:53,800
this crisis is over, there will be

840
00:37:53,800 --> 00:37:56,800
a sudden surge of inflation.

841
00:37:56,800 --> 00:38:00,800
And let me come back to that.

842
00:38:00,800 --> 00:38:02,800
Basically, what the Fed has a new

843
00:38:02,800 --> 00:38:04,800
tool is, if that happens to raise the

844
00:38:04,800 --> 00:38:08,800
interest on reserves, and that

845
00:38:08,800 --> 00:38:12,800
will help contract the economy

846
00:38:12,800 --> 00:38:15,800
instantly, very rapidly.

847
00:38:15,800 --> 00:38:17,800
The way the Fed controls the

848
00:38:17,800 --> 00:38:19,800
Federal funds rate, and has been doing

849
00:38:19,800 --> 00:38:22,300
for years, is by buying and

850
00:38:22,300 --> 00:38:24,800
selling Treasury bills on the open market,

851
00:38:24,800 --> 00:38:31,800
by affecting the supply and demand for short-term credit.

852
00:38:31,800 --> 00:38:33,300
And that indirectly, they don't

853
00:38:33,300 --> 00:38:34,800
actually deal in the Federal funds

854
00:38:34,800 --> 00:38:35,800
market.

855
00:38:35,800 --> 00:38:37,800
They deal in the Treasury bill market.

856
00:38:37,800 --> 00:38:40,800
But since those markets are interlinked, they indirectly

857
00:38:40,800 --> 00:38:44,800
target the Federal funds rate.

858
00:38:44,800 --> 00:38:46,300
So, that's the old way

859
00:38:46,300 --> 00:38:47,800
of trading in the Federal funds

860
00:38:47,800 --> 00:38:49,300
market, not in the Federal

861
00:38:49,300 --> 00:38:50,800
funds market, trading in the short-term

862
00:38:50,800 --> 00:38:54,800
Treasury market through the New York Fed.

863
00:38:54,800 --> 00:38:56,800
But now they have a new tool.

864
00:38:56,800 --> 00:39:02,800
So, we're entering a whole new regime of monetary policy.

865
00:39:02,800 --> 00:39:03,800
Okay.

866
00:39:03,800 --> 00:39:06,800
So, I wanted to talk about

867
00:39:06,800 --> 00:39:09,800
reserve requirements a little bit more,

868
00:39:09,800 --> 00:39:17,800
and what those are.

869
00:39:17,800 --> 00:39:22,300
So, the Federal Reserve has the

870
00:39:22,300 --> 00:39:26,800
authority to set the amount of reserves

871
00:39:26,800 --> 00:39:29,800
that a bank holds.

872
00:39:29,800 --> 00:39:31,800
I wanted to make clear

873
00:39:31,800 --> 00:39:33,800
the distinction between a reserve requirement

874
00:39:33,800 --> 00:39:35,800
and capital requirements.

875
00:39:35,800 --> 00:39:45,800
So, okay, the Federal Reserve

876
00:39:45,800 --> 00:39:55,800
has what's called Regulation D,

877
00:39:55,800 --> 00:39:59,800
which specifies how much banks have to

878
00:39:59,800 --> 00:40:03,800
hold as a function of their liabilities.

879
00:40:03,800 --> 00:40:14,800
And as of right now, the

880
00:40:14,800 --> 00:40:25,800
reserve requirements are 10% of transaction accounts.

881
00:40:25,800 --> 00:40:29,800
Okay, that means that a bank has to

882
00:40:29,800 --> 00:40:33,800
total up all of the transaction accounts, and that

883
00:40:33,800 --> 00:40:36,800
consists of checking accounts.

884
00:40:36,800 --> 00:40:42,800
What are the now accounts and ATS accounts.

885
00:40:42,800 --> 00:40:45,800
Transactions accounts are accounts like checks that

886
00:40:45,800 --> 00:40:48,800
people have a deposit in the bank that

887
00:40:48,800 --> 00:40:50,800
they'll use for spending.

888
00:40:50,800 --> 00:40:53,800
And those are instantly withdrawable.

889
00:40:53,800 --> 00:40:54,800
Okay.

890
00:40:54,800 --> 00:40:59,800
In contrast, there's something called time deposits.

891
00:40:59,800 --> 00:41:02,800
Those are savings accounts.

892
00:41:03,800 --> 00:41:06,800
And the bank does not have

893
00:41:06,800 --> 00:41:09,800
to give you the money immediately.

894
00:41:09,800 --> 00:41:11,300
In other words, if you go

895
00:41:11,300 --> 00:41:12,800
to your checking account and say,

896
00:41:12,800 --> 00:41:15,300
I want my money, it's a transaction account,

897
00:41:15,300 --> 00:41:17,800
they have to give it to you instantly.

898
00:41:17,800 --> 00:41:19,800
That's the rule.

899
00:41:19,800 --> 00:41:21,800
But if you go to your saving

900
00:41:21,800 --> 00:41:23,800
account, they can stall, because it's a,

901
00:41:23,800 --> 00:41:25,300
you might not have noticed it,

902
00:41:25,300 --> 00:41:26,800
but it's in the fine print somewhere.

903
00:41:26,800 --> 00:41:28,800
This is a time deposit.

904
00:41:28,800 --> 00:41:32,800
So, the Fed is not worried about time deposits.

905
00:41:32,800 --> 00:41:36,800
Here, we're talking about reserve requirements.

906
00:41:36,800 --> 00:41:40,300
Reserve requirements are still based on the

907
00:41:40,300 --> 00:41:43,800
old theory that we're trying to prevent bank

908
00:41:43,800 --> 00:41:44,800
runs.

909
00:41:44,800 --> 00:41:45,800
Okay?

910
00:41:45,800 --> 00:41:48,300
And so, we don't want people to, we don't

911
00:41:48,300 --> 00:41:50,800
want there to be a run on banks, where people

912
00:41:50,800 --> 00:41:53,800
panic and try to withdraw their money all at once.

913
00:41:53,800 --> 00:41:55,300
So, we want to make

914
00:41:55,300 --> 00:41:56,800
sure that banks have enough reserves.

915
00:41:56,800 --> 00:42:00,800
And the Fed currently thinks 10% ought to be enough.

916
00:42:00,800 --> 00:42:01,800
For time deposits, it's zero.

917
00:42:01,800 --> 00:42:04,800
You don't have to put any

918
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:07,800
money on reserve for time deposits.

919
00:42:07,800 --> 00:42:09,800
Why does the Fed think that?

920
00:42:09,800 --> 00:42:12,300
Because you've got 60, 90 days or

921
00:42:12,300 --> 00:42:14,800
a year to pay the person back.

922
00:42:14,800 --> 00:42:16,800
So, they can't run on you.

923
00:42:16,800 --> 00:42:19,300
So, we don't require any

924
00:42:19,300 --> 00:42:21,800
reserve requirements against time deposits.

925
00:42:21,800 --> 00:42:25,800
But it's 10% for transactions account, which

926
00:42:25,800 --> 00:42:29,800
is substantial, because they're still worried about this.

927
00:42:29,800 --> 00:42:30,800
Okay.

928
00:42:30,800 --> 00:42:33,800
So, this is the situation.

929
00:42:33,800 --> 00:42:37,300
We used to talk, emphasize in lectures

930
00:42:37,300 --> 00:42:40,800
about central banking, the so-called money multiplier.

931
00:42:40,800 --> 00:42:41,800
Okay?

932
00:42:41,800 --> 00:42:45,300
The money multiplier, well, it's complicated, but

933
00:42:45,300 --> 00:42:48,800
the simplest thing is, it might be one

934
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:50,800
over the reserve requirement.

935
00:42:50,800 --> 00:42:53,800
It's not exactly that, but if the

936
00:42:53,800 --> 00:42:56,800
reserve requirement is 10%, then the total amount

937
00:42:56,800 --> 00:42:57,800
of deposits that banks can issue

938
00:42:57,800 --> 00:42:58,800
is one over the reserve requirement.

939
00:42:58,800 --> 00:42:59,800
Okay?

940
00:42:59,800 --> 00:43:03,300
So, the total amount of deposits that banks can

941
00:43:03,300 --> 00:43:06,800
issue is going to be 10 times the amount

942
00:43:06,800 --> 00:43:10,800
of currency and deposits they have at the Federal Reserve.

943
00:43:10,800 --> 00:43:13,800
So, that means the reserve requirements would

944
00:43:13,800 --> 00:43:16,800
fix the money supply under the old theory.

945
00:43:16,800 --> 00:43:21,300
Because, you know, the high-powered money is

946
00:43:21,300 --> 00:43:25,800
the currency plus deposits at the Federal Reserve.

947
00:43:25,800 --> 00:43:26,800
That's reserves.

948
00:43:26,800 --> 00:43:28,300
And if the reserve requirement is 10%,

949
00:43:28,300 --> 00:43:29,800
and banks want to just meet the requirements,

950
00:43:29,800 --> 00:43:33,300
and nothing more, they're going to have 10

951
00:43:33,300 --> 00:43:36,800
times as much deposits, right, as there are reserves.

952
00:43:36,800 --> 00:43:39,800
I'm oversimplifying the money multiplier.

953
00:43:39,800 --> 00:43:42,300
But I'm telling you that at the

954
00:43:42,300 --> 00:43:44,800
moment in history, it's irrelevant, because banks are

955
00:43:44,800 --> 00:43:47,800
holding excess reserves.

956
00:43:47,800 --> 00:43:52,800
The world has changed.

957
00:43:52,800 --> 00:43:55,800
Just a few years ago, before this financial

958
00:43:55,800 --> 00:43:58,800
crisis, banks didn't want to hold excess reserves.

959
00:43:58,800 --> 00:44:01,800
And so, there were hardly any excess reserves.

960
00:44:01,800 --> 00:44:02,800
Why?

961
00:44:02,800 --> 00:44:05,800
Because they don't get any interest on them, all right?

962
00:44:05,800 --> 00:44:08,800
And so, banks didn't hold excess reserves.

963
00:44:08,800 --> 00:44:11,300
So, the money market multiplier theory would

964
00:44:11,300 --> 00:44:13,800
work, because the amount of reserve was just

965
00:44:13,800 --> 00:44:16,800
about exactly equal to 10% of transactions.

966
00:44:16,800 --> 00:44:20,800
I'm oversimplifying, but something like that was true.

967
00:44:20,800 --> 00:44:24,800
But now, they're paying 25 basis points on reserves.

968
00:44:24,800 --> 00:44:26,800
So, that's a lot more than you

969
00:44:26,800 --> 00:44:28,800
can get investing in the Federal Funds Market.

970
00:44:28,800 --> 00:44:32,800
And banks are just perfectly happy to hold excess reserves.

971
00:44:32,800 --> 00:44:36,300
So, the excess reserves now are

972
00:44:36,300 --> 00:44:39,800
over, I think it's 1.2 trillion.

973
00:44:39,800 --> 00:44:50,800
It's huge, because of interest on reserves.

974
00:44:50,800 --> 00:44:54,800
I don't have the exact number, but something

975
00:44:54,800 --> 00:44:58,800
has fundamentally changed in just the last few years.

976
00:44:58,800 --> 00:45:03,800
So, reserve requirements, they must hold for some banks.

977
00:45:03,800 --> 00:45:05,300
But for most banks, they don't

978
00:45:05,300 --> 00:45:06,800
even look at reserve requirements anymore.

979
00:45:06,800 --> 00:45:08,800
What do I care?

980
00:45:08,800 --> 00:45:11,800
I'm so happy to hold reserves.

981
00:45:11,800 --> 00:45:13,800
I'll hold way more than they require.

982
00:45:13,800 --> 00:45:20,800
So, reserve requirements are non-binding for most banks now.

983
00:45:20,800 --> 00:45:23,800
So, it's a different world.

984
00:45:23,800 --> 00:45:26,800
This brings us then to capital requirements.

985
00:45:26,800 --> 00:45:27,800
Capital requirements.

986
00:45:27,800 --> 00:45:32,800
So, in the world, history has always changed.

987
00:45:32,800 --> 00:45:34,800
The world, as of a few

988
00:45:34,800 --> 00:45:36,800
years ago, everyone emphasized reserve requirements.

989
00:45:36,800 --> 00:45:39,800
And those were the requirements that

990
00:45:39,800 --> 00:45:42,800
had as its motive preventing bank runs.

991
00:45:42,800 --> 00:45:45,800
But we're not going to have a bank run now,

992
00:45:45,800 --> 00:45:48,800
when these banks have over a trillion dollars just sitting there.

993
00:45:48,800 --> 00:45:50,800
They're holding so much.

994
00:45:50,800 --> 00:45:53,800
It's not an issue right now.

995
00:45:53,800 --> 00:45:55,800
So, something else has taken the center stage.

996
00:45:55,800 --> 00:45:59,300
And that is capital requirements,

997
00:45:59,300 --> 00:46:02,800
which we talked about last time.

998
00:46:02,800 --> 00:46:09,800
So, capital requirements are

999
00:46:09,800 --> 00:46:16,800
different from reserve requirements.

1000
00:46:16,800 --> 00:46:20,300
I just defined, reserve requirements were

1001
00:46:20,300 --> 00:46:23,800
a fraction of the transaction accounts.

1002
00:46:23,800 --> 00:46:24,800
They were defined by a liability.

1003
00:46:25,800 --> 00:46:28,800
A transaction account is like a checking account.

1004
00:46:28,800 --> 00:46:32,800
It's some money that the bank owes to other people.

1005
00:46:32,800 --> 00:46:36,800
And we have this requirement that

1006
00:46:36,800 --> 00:46:40,800
10% of that is the reserve requirement.

1007
00:46:40,800 --> 00:46:43,800
But capital requirements are different.

1008
00:46:43,800 --> 00:46:47,800
And they're more likely to be binding these days.

1009
00:46:47,800 --> 00:46:50,800
And these were emphasized in Basel

1010
00:46:50,800 --> 00:46:53,800
III, which we talked about before.

1011
00:46:53,800 --> 00:46:54,800
So, it's a different thing.

1012
00:46:54,800 --> 00:46:56,800
And it's a different thing

1013
00:46:56,800 --> 00:46:58,800
than what we talked about before.

1014
00:46:58,800 --> 00:47:09,800
So, Basel III is not yet in force.

1015
00:47:09,800 --> 00:47:11,800
It's going to take a long time.

1016
00:47:11,800 --> 00:47:15,800
Basel III has a phase-in period that is

1017
00:47:15,800 --> 00:47:19,800
going to take until, I think, it's 2019.

1018
00:47:19,800 --> 00:47:21,300
But there's a lot of talk now

1019
00:47:21,300 --> 00:47:22,800
about trying to get Basel III phased in.

1020
00:47:22,800 --> 00:47:31,800
So, remember, we talked about risk-weighted assets.

1021
00:47:31,800 --> 00:47:37,300
Now it is banks have to hold

1022
00:47:37,300 --> 00:47:42,800
capital as a fraction of their risk-weighted assets.

1023
00:47:42,800 --> 00:47:45,800
So, right now, the countries of the world, they've agreed.

1024
00:47:45,800 --> 00:47:47,800
The G20 countries have agreed on Basel III.

1025
00:47:47,800 --> 00:47:49,800
But each country has to decide

1026
00:47:49,800 --> 00:47:51,800
on the implementation of Basel III.

1027
00:47:52,800 --> 00:47:55,300
The United States, in particular,

1028
00:47:55,300 --> 00:47:57,800
is having problems with Basel III,

1029
00:47:57,800 --> 00:48:09,800
because Basel III refers to credit ratings in many places.

1030
00:48:09,800 --> 00:48:20,800
But Dodd-Frank, the Dodd-Frank Act, Act of 2010,

1031
00:48:22,800 --> 00:48:25,800
abolishes credit ratings.

1032
00:48:25,800 --> 00:48:29,300
The government will no longer make any

1033
00:48:29,300 --> 00:48:32,800
use in any regulation of credit ratings.

1034
00:48:32,800 --> 00:48:34,800
Remember what credit ratings are.

1035
00:48:34,800 --> 00:48:38,300
Moody's and Standard & Poor's are

1036
00:48:38,300 --> 00:48:41,800
the two best-known credit rating agencies.

1037
00:48:41,800 --> 00:48:49,800
The government, the SEC, starting in 1975,

1038
00:48:49,800 --> 00:48:51,800
defined what they called NRA.

1039
00:48:51,800 --> 00:48:55,800
N-R-S-R-O's.

1040
00:48:55,800 --> 00:48:59,800
That's Nationally Recognized

1041
00:48:59,800 --> 00:49:03,800
Statistical Rating Organization.

1042
00:49:03,800 --> 00:49:05,800
All right?

1043
00:49:05,800 --> 00:49:11,800
And that included Moody's, which was founded in about 1900,

1044
00:49:11,800 --> 00:49:14,300
and Standard & Poor's, which was

1045
00:49:14,300 --> 00:49:16,800
a result of the merger of

1046
00:49:16,800 --> 00:49:20,800
Standard Statistical Association and Poor's,

1047
00:49:20,800 --> 00:49:22,800
a little bit after 1900.

1048
00:49:22,800 --> 00:49:25,300
They're venerable old institutions that

1049
00:49:25,300 --> 00:49:27,800
give a risk rating for securities.

1050
00:49:27,800 --> 00:49:30,300
For example, Moody's will give its

1051
00:49:30,300 --> 00:49:32,800
best securities a triple A rating.

1052
00:49:32,800 --> 00:49:33,800
Okay?

1053
00:49:33,800 --> 00:49:36,800
That means Moody's thinks they'll never default.

1054
00:49:36,800 --> 00:49:42,800
Yale University is rated triple A by Moody's, for example.

1055
00:49:42,800 --> 00:49:45,800
But they go, if they don't like you

1056
00:49:45,800 --> 00:49:48,800
quite as much, they'll downrate you to double A.

1057
00:49:48,800 --> 00:49:51,300
Or if they don't like

1058
00:49:51,300 --> 00:49:53,800
you even more, you're only A.

1059
00:49:53,800 --> 00:49:56,800
And then, God forbid, you go down into the B's.

1060
00:49:56,800 --> 00:49:59,300
It's like, in fact, Moody, John Moody

1061
00:49:59,300 --> 00:50:01,800
in his book acknowledges that it was just,

1062
00:50:01,800 --> 00:50:03,800
he took the same grading

1063
00:50:03,800 --> 00:50:05,800
system that he got in college.

1064
00:50:05,800 --> 00:50:06,800
I don't know why, it's a little different.

1065
00:50:06,800 --> 00:50:08,800
You don't get a triple A grade here, do you?

1066
00:50:08,800 --> 00:50:10,800
I've never given a triple A.

1067
00:50:10,800 --> 00:50:13,800
But that's how Moody saw it.

1068
00:50:13,800 --> 00:50:16,800
And so, it's like letter grades to securities.

1069
00:50:16,800 --> 00:50:19,800
Well, Moody did that in 1900.

1070
00:50:19,800 --> 00:50:22,300
And if you read his

1071
00:50:22,300 --> 00:50:24,800
autobiography, people said, you're crazy.

1072
00:50:24,800 --> 00:50:27,800
How can you give a grade to a security?

1073
00:50:27,800 --> 00:50:29,800
It seemed like a wild idea.

1074
00:50:29,800 --> 00:50:30,800
But he stuck with it.

1075
00:50:30,800 --> 00:50:32,800
And over the years, people began

1076
00:50:32,800 --> 00:50:34,800
to believe in them more and more.

1077
00:50:34,800 --> 00:50:37,800
So, it led to an idea that

1078
00:50:37,800 --> 00:50:40,800
we fully understand the risk of securities.

1079
00:50:40,800 --> 00:50:42,800
And so, there was a

1080
00:50:42,800 --> 00:50:44,800
set of, a complacency set in.

1081
00:50:44,800 --> 00:50:46,800
And the government started,

1082
00:50:46,800 --> 00:50:49,800
to recognize these NRSROs as if

1083
00:50:49,800 --> 00:50:52,800
they were proclaimers of God's truth.

1084
00:50:52,800 --> 00:50:54,800
And they started all kinds of regulations,

1085
00:50:54,800 --> 00:50:56,800
said what your capital had to be,

1086
00:50:56,800 --> 00:51:01,800
depending on the rating of various assets you hold.

1087
00:51:01,800 --> 00:51:03,800
But the whole thing collapsed

1088
00:51:03,800 --> 00:51:05,800
in the recent financial crisis,

1089
00:51:05,800 --> 00:51:10,800
because some triple A securities lost almost everything.

1090
00:51:10,800 --> 00:51:14,800
So, the rating agencies made a big problem, issue.

1091
00:51:14,800 --> 00:51:16,800
And so, Congress,

1092
00:51:16,800 --> 00:51:20,800
has now said, nobody can make any

1093
00:51:20,800 --> 00:51:24,800
regulation based on ratings of the NRSROs.

1094
00:51:24,800 --> 00:51:28,800
But Basel III people didn't get the message.

1095
00:51:28,800 --> 00:51:30,800
Well, they're not America, they're international.

1096
00:51:30,800 --> 00:51:32,800
And they still believe in them.

1097
00:51:32,800 --> 00:51:35,800
I think it's a little bit

1098
00:51:35,800 --> 00:51:38,800
difficult to know how to handle risk.

1099
00:51:38,800 --> 00:51:40,800
This is the fundamental problem.

1100
00:51:40,800 --> 00:51:42,800
And it would be nice if

1101
00:51:42,800 --> 00:51:44,800
Moody's and S&P could tell us.

1102
00:51:44,800 --> 00:51:47,800
But the problem is that they missed this whole crisis.

1103
00:51:47,800 --> 00:51:49,800
They didn't see it coming.

1104
00:51:49,800 --> 00:51:51,800
And why didn't they?

1105
00:51:51,800 --> 00:51:53,800
Well, that's a deep issue.

1106
00:51:53,800 --> 00:51:55,800
That's what people are wrestling with right now.

1107
00:51:55,800 --> 00:51:58,800
So, the U.S. has to figure out what to do,

1108
00:51:58,800 --> 00:52:01,300
to reinterpret the Basel III

1109
00:52:01,300 --> 00:52:03,800
recommendations for the United States,

1110
00:52:03,800 --> 00:52:06,800
without using any reference to Moody's and S&P.

1111
00:52:06,800 --> 00:52:09,800
What will probably happen, I think,

1112
00:52:09,800 --> 00:52:11,300
is that the banks will have

1113
00:52:11,300 --> 00:52:12,800
to have their own risk committees,

1114
00:52:12,800 --> 00:52:14,800
and they will have to come up with a plan,

1115
00:52:14,800 --> 00:52:16,300
and they will have to come

1116
00:52:16,300 --> 00:52:17,800
up with their own assessments of risk,

1117
00:52:17,800 --> 00:52:19,800
and they'll be responsible for those.

1118
00:52:19,800 --> 00:52:21,800
But what will really happen is,

1119
00:52:21,800 --> 00:52:23,800
they'll just look at the Moody's and S&P ratings,

1120
00:52:23,800 --> 00:52:26,800
and it'll be rubber stamping them.

1121
00:52:26,800 --> 00:52:29,800
So, I don't think that, there's a whole question,

1122
00:52:29,800 --> 00:52:32,300
whether Dodd-Frank will be

1123
00:52:32,300 --> 00:52:34,800
effective in reducing our requirement.

1124
00:52:34,800 --> 00:52:36,800
But I wanted to go

1125
00:52:36,800 --> 00:52:38,800
over the capital requirements once again,

1126
00:52:38,800 --> 00:52:42,800
because now they're increasingly important.

1127
00:52:42,800 --> 00:52:44,800
And I wanted to go through,

1128
00:52:44,800 --> 00:52:48,800
just a simple example of capital requirements,

1129
00:52:48,800 --> 00:52:54,800
so that you'll understand them.

1130
00:52:54,800 --> 00:52:57,800
And I don't think the general

1131
00:52:57,800 --> 00:53:00,800
public understands them very well at all.

1132
00:53:00,800 --> 00:53:01,800
Okay.

1133
00:53:01,800 --> 00:53:09,800
And so, this is old accounting, or old finance.

1134
00:53:09,800 --> 00:53:12,800
These requirements go back 100 years.

1135
00:53:12,800 --> 00:53:14,800
I'm going to talk in very simple terms about them.

1136
00:53:14,800 --> 00:53:16,800
I'm not going to go over-simplifying.

1137
00:53:16,800 --> 00:53:19,800
Basel III is a complicated new agreement.

1138
00:53:19,800 --> 00:53:21,800
It has a lot of ins and outs.

1139
00:53:21,800 --> 00:53:23,800
But I'm just going to over-simplify it,

1140
00:53:23,800 --> 00:53:28,800
and talk about the Basel III common equity requirements.

1141
00:53:28,800 --> 00:53:31,800
I'm going to tell a

1142
00:53:31,800 --> 00:53:34,800
little story about founding a bank.

1143
00:53:34,800 --> 00:53:37,800
And let's just understand how capital requirements work.

1144
00:53:37,800 --> 00:53:42,800
So, imagine that you decided to found a bank.

1145
00:53:42,800 --> 00:53:45,800
And in developed countries of the world today,

1146
00:53:45,800 --> 00:53:46,800
you can do this.

1147
00:53:46,800 --> 00:53:48,800
You can set up your own bank.

1148
00:53:48,800 --> 00:53:49,800
The only problem you have,

1149
00:53:49,800 --> 00:53:50,800
you have to get a charter.

1150
00:53:50,800 --> 00:53:51,800
You have to apply for a charter.

1151
00:53:51,800 --> 00:53:53,300
You have to decide whether you're

1152
00:53:53,300 --> 00:53:54,800
a national bank in the United States,

1153
00:53:54,800 --> 00:53:56,800
or some other kind of bank.

1154
00:53:56,800 --> 00:53:59,800
But you get a charter, and you open your doors.

1155
00:53:59,800 --> 00:54:01,300
Now, you've got to

1156
00:54:01,300 --> 00:54:02,800
start complying with capital requirements.

1157
00:54:02,800 --> 00:54:07,800
But let's say you do that, all right?

1158
00:54:07,800 --> 00:54:10,800
You find that there's an empty bank building downtown.

1159
00:54:10,800 --> 00:54:11,800
All right?

1160
00:54:11,800 --> 00:54:13,300
So, you go through the paperwork,

1161
00:54:13,300 --> 00:54:14,800
and you set up your bank,

1162
00:54:14,800 --> 00:54:16,800
and you open the doors.

1163
00:54:16,800 --> 00:54:18,300
And now, we have one

1164
00:54:18,300 --> 00:54:19,800
of those windows with a teller,

1165
00:54:19,800 --> 00:54:21,800
and inviting deposits, okay?

1166
00:54:21,800 --> 00:54:24,800
So, I'm going to tell a story, which is over-simplified,

1167
00:54:24,800 --> 00:54:27,800
and maybe a little bit, let's

1168
00:54:27,800 --> 00:54:30,800
say that you open your doors,

1169
00:54:30,800 --> 00:54:33,300
and someone walks in with

1170
00:54:33,300 --> 00:54:35,800
$100 and deposits it, all right?

1171
00:54:35,800 --> 00:54:37,800
So, this is your bank.

1172
00:54:37,800 --> 00:54:39,800
Or I'll say, my bank, okay?

1173
00:54:39,800 --> 00:54:40,800
All right?

1174
00:54:41,800 --> 00:54:43,800
So, you've got a bank.

1175
00:54:43,800 --> 00:54:45,800
And the bank has, now, you

1176
00:54:45,800 --> 00:54:47,800
probably, I think regulators would require

1177
00:54:47,800 --> 00:54:49,800
you come up with some capital first.

1178
00:54:49,800 --> 00:54:51,800
But I'm going to tell the story.

1179
00:54:51,800 --> 00:54:52,800
It seems to be a nicer story.

1180
00:54:52,800 --> 00:54:53,800
You start out with deposit.

1181
00:54:53,800 --> 00:54:55,800
Assume your regulators allowed you to start.

1182
00:54:55,800 --> 00:54:57,800
So, somebody walks in and deposits.

1183
00:54:57,800 --> 00:55:01,800
And here's your assets, and here's your liabilities, okay?

1184
00:55:01,800 --> 00:55:04,800
Left side is assets, right side is liabilities.

1185
00:55:04,800 --> 00:55:07,300
So, someone deposits $100 in

1186
00:55:07,300 --> 00:55:09,800
cash to your bank, all right?

1187
00:55:09,800 --> 00:55:14,800
And you've got an asset, now, of $100, okay?

1188
00:55:14,800 --> 00:55:19,800
And you have a liability, now, of $100.

1189
00:55:19,800 --> 00:55:22,300
If that's a savings account,

1190
00:55:22,300 --> 00:55:24,800
you don't have any reserve requirements.

1191
00:55:24,800 --> 00:55:27,800
If it's a transactions account, you have to hold $10.

1192
00:55:27,800 --> 00:55:28,800
I've got it, right?

1193
00:55:28,800 --> 00:55:31,300
I've got $100 sitting in my vault,

1194
00:55:31,300 --> 00:55:33,800
because the guy just gave me $100.

1195
00:55:33,800 --> 00:55:37,800
So, I'm satisfying both my reserve requirements and my,

1196
00:55:37,800 --> 00:55:39,800
now, am I satisfying my capital requirements?

1197
00:55:39,800 --> 00:55:42,800
Well, I have to calculate risk-weighted assets, all right?

1198
00:55:42,800 --> 00:55:45,800
Remember, every kind of asset has its risk.

1199
00:55:45,800 --> 00:55:47,800
What about cash?

1200
00:55:47,800 --> 00:55:49,800
Well, cash has a zero risk.

1201
00:55:49,800 --> 00:55:52,800
So, it has a zero risk weighting.

1202
00:55:52,800 --> 00:56:01,800
So, right now, my risk-weighted assets equals zero, okay?

1203
00:56:01,800 --> 00:56:03,800
Now, okay, I've got to satisfy

1204
00:56:03,800 --> 00:56:05,800
all the regulations that are on me.

1205
00:56:05,800 --> 00:56:08,800
I'm satisfying my reserve requirements, right?

1206
00:56:08,800 --> 00:56:10,800
I've got my reserve, I've got $100.

1207
00:56:10,800 --> 00:56:16,800
This is cash in my safe.

1208
00:56:16,800 --> 00:56:19,800
And this is a liability.

1209
00:56:19,800 --> 00:56:23,800
I owe $100 in the form of

1210
00:56:23,800 --> 00:56:27,800
a, let's say, it's a transaction account.

1211
00:56:27,800 --> 00:56:30,800
Whenever this person comes back,

1212
00:56:30,800 --> 00:56:33,800
I've got to pay $100, okay?

1213
00:56:33,800 --> 00:56:37,800
All right, so, my reserve requirements are satisfied.

1214
00:56:37,800 --> 00:56:41,800
Because I've got more than 10% of my transactions account.

1215
00:56:41,800 --> 00:56:43,800
My risk-weighted assets are zero.

1216
00:56:43,800 --> 00:56:48,800
Now, Basel III says that you have to

1217
00:56:48,800 --> 00:56:53,800
hold 4.5% of your risk-weighted assets as capital.

1218
00:56:53,800 --> 00:56:56,800
But now, wait a minute, I have a problem.

1219
00:56:56,800 --> 00:56:59,800
What is my capital here?

1220
00:56:59,800 --> 00:57:01,800
I don't have any capital.

1221
00:57:01,800 --> 00:57:04,300
I've just opened my doors

1222
00:57:04,300 --> 00:57:06,800
and I've got $100 in assets.

1223
00:57:06,800 --> 00:57:09,800
I've got $100 in liabilities.

1224
00:57:09,800 --> 00:57:12,300
Everything looks okay from reserve

1225
00:57:12,300 --> 00:57:14,800
requirement, but there's no capital.

1226
00:57:14,800 --> 00:57:16,800
So, what is capital?

1227
00:57:16,800 --> 00:57:21,800
I have to issue shares to come up with capital.

1228
00:57:21,800 --> 00:57:32,800
And I'm going to need 4.5% common equity requirement.

1229
00:57:36,800 --> 00:57:41,800
And they also have something called a capital

1230
00:57:41,800 --> 00:57:46,800
conservation buffer, which is another 2.5% plus 2.5%,

1231
00:57:46,800 --> 00:57:49,300
which you don't absolutely have to hold, but

1232
00:57:49,300 --> 00:57:51,800
if you don't hold it, you're subject to restrictions.

1233
00:57:51,800 --> 00:57:53,800
So, I'm going to add these.

1234
00:57:53,800 --> 00:57:55,800
This is the capital conservation buffer.

1235
00:57:55,800 --> 00:58:00,800
I've got to hold 7% under Basel III.

1236
00:58:00,800 --> 00:58:03,800
And I don't have any capital here.

1237
00:58:03,800 --> 00:58:04,800
All right.

1238
00:58:04,800 --> 00:58:05,800
So, what do I do?

1239
00:58:05,800 --> 00:58:07,800
I'm not in compliance.

1240
00:58:07,800 --> 00:58:09,800
So, I've got to do something.

1241
00:58:09,800 --> 00:58:11,800
I've got to raise capital.

1242
00:58:11,800 --> 00:58:15,800
So, what I can do is I can issue shares.

1243
00:58:15,800 --> 00:58:20,800
I have to find, sell shares in the business.

1244
00:58:20,800 --> 00:58:24,300
So, all I need to do is

1245
00:58:24,300 --> 00:58:27,800
sell 7, well, okay, what would it be?

1246
00:58:27,800 --> 00:58:31,800
Let's say I put in, I issue $20 in shares.

1247
00:58:31,800 --> 00:58:33,800
Okay.

1248
00:58:33,800 --> 00:58:36,800
And then that means someone gives me more

1249
00:58:36,800 --> 00:58:39,800
cash, right, because someone paid for the shares.

1250
00:58:39,800 --> 00:58:43,800
So, I've now got $120.

1251
00:58:43,800 --> 00:58:46,800
Well, I add another plus 20.

1252
00:58:46,800 --> 00:58:49,300
So, it's a total assets

1253
00:58:49,300 --> 00:58:51,800
of 120 all held in cash.

1254
00:58:51,800 --> 00:58:52,800
Okay.

1255
00:58:52,800 --> 00:58:55,300
Now, I've got, now this is

1256
00:58:55,300 --> 00:58:57,800
not a different kind of liability.

1257
00:58:57,800 --> 00:59:01,800
This is common equity.

1258
00:59:03,800 --> 00:59:09,300
And the regulators make a big distinction between this kind of

1259
00:59:09,300 --> 00:59:14,800
liability, the transaction account, and this kind of liability, the $20.

1260
00:59:14,800 --> 00:59:16,800
Why do they make a big distinction?

1261
00:59:16,800 --> 00:59:20,300
Because this guy can come to the window anytime and

1262
00:59:20,300 --> 00:59:23,800
you've got to give them $100, whenever this person asks.

1263
00:59:23,800 --> 00:59:26,800
The shareholders have no demand on you at all.

1264
00:59:26,800 --> 00:59:28,800
They own a share of the company.

1265
00:59:28,800 --> 00:59:30,300
You don't, they can't come

1266
00:59:30,300 --> 00:59:31,800
to the window and demand anything.

1267
00:59:31,800 --> 00:59:32,800
You can just send them away.

1268
00:59:32,800 --> 00:59:38,800
The only deal you have with shareholders is that all shareholders will

1269
00:59:38,800 --> 00:59:44,800
receive an equal dividend if the board of directors decides to vote dividend.

1270
00:59:44,800 --> 00:59:46,800
They're shareholders in the company.

1271
00:59:46,800 --> 00:59:48,800
But they can't run the bank.

1272
00:59:48,800 --> 00:59:50,800
The shareholders can't show up at the window.

1273
00:59:50,800 --> 00:59:52,800
So, there's no risk of bank runs for them.

1274
00:59:52,800 --> 00:59:55,800
And there's no risk of problems because if anything goes

1275
00:59:55,800 --> 00:59:58,800
bad, you just tell the common equity guys, you're out.

1276
00:59:58,800 --> 01:00:00,800
You lost.

1277
01:00:00,800 --> 01:00:01,800
Okay.

1278
01:00:01,800 --> 01:00:04,800
So, now, what are my risk-weighted assets?

1279
01:00:04,800 --> 01:00:05,800
They're still zero.

1280
01:00:05,800 --> 01:00:08,800
I've got $120 in cash, zero.

1281
01:00:08,800 --> 01:00:12,800
I don't have any, 7% times zero is zero.

1282
01:00:12,800 --> 01:00:15,800
So, now, I'm in great shape.

1283
01:00:15,800 --> 01:00:18,800
I've satisfied both my reserve requirements and

1284
01:00:18,800 --> 01:00:21,800
I've satisfied my Basel III capital requirements.

1285
01:00:21,800 --> 01:00:24,800
Okay.

1286
01:00:24,800 --> 01:00:26,800
So, what do we do next?

1287
01:00:26,800 --> 01:00:27,800
We created a bank.

1288
01:00:27,800 --> 01:00:29,800
And there's no interest paid on these two.

1289
01:00:29,800 --> 01:00:32,800
And there's no interest paid on these checking accounts.

1290
01:00:32,800 --> 01:00:34,800
And we're not earning any interest.

1291
01:00:34,800 --> 01:00:36,800
We've got a bank and we're satisfied in the requirements.

1292
01:00:36,800 --> 01:00:38,800
But nobody is making any money.

1293
01:00:38,800 --> 01:00:41,800
So, we've got to do something to make money.

1294
01:00:41,800 --> 01:00:44,800
So, what I'm going to do is I'm going

1295
01:00:44,800 --> 01:00:47,800
to, let's say we get our board of directors meeting.

1296
01:00:47,800 --> 01:00:49,800
The board of directors is

1297
01:00:49,800 --> 01:00:51,800
elected by the common equity shareholders.

1298
01:00:51,800 --> 01:00:56,800
And we decide, let's make some corporate loans.

1299
01:00:56,800 --> 01:01:00,300
So, why don't we lend out all of this $100 that

1300
01:01:00,300 --> 01:01:03,800
was cash and lend it to some business as a corporate loan.

1301
01:01:03,800 --> 01:01:04,800
And then we'll charge them interest.

1302
01:01:04,800 --> 01:01:06,800
Now, we're going to start making money.

1303
01:01:06,800 --> 01:01:09,800
So, this is no longer cash in safe.

1304
01:01:09,800 --> 01:01:13,800
This is corporate loans.

1305
01:01:13,800 --> 01:01:16,800
Loans to corporations to do business.

1306
01:01:16,800 --> 01:01:17,800
All right.

1307
01:01:17,800 --> 01:01:20,800
So, there we are.

1308
01:01:20,800 --> 01:01:22,800
Our balance sheet balances.

1309
01:01:22,800 --> 01:01:24,800
Everything looks fine.

1310
01:01:24,800 --> 01:01:26,800
But what is our risk-weighted asset now?

1311
01:01:26,800 --> 01:01:30,800
Well, you remember, corporate loans get a 100% risk-weight.

1312
01:01:30,800 --> 01:01:34,300
Because under Basel, going back to Basel I,

1313
01:01:34,300 --> 01:01:37,800
they always thought corporate loans are risky assets.

1314
01:01:37,800 --> 01:01:42,800
So, my risk-weighted assets are now $100.

1315
01:01:42,800 --> 01:01:44,800
Okay?

1316
01:01:44,800 --> 01:01:47,800
And 7% of $100 is $7.

1317
01:01:47,800 --> 01:01:49,800
So, hey, I'm doing fine.

1318
01:01:49,800 --> 01:01:51,800
Now, we're capitalized enough.

1319
01:01:51,800 --> 01:01:53,800
We're in business.

1320
01:01:53,800 --> 01:01:55,800
We're satisfying both our reserve requirements.

1321
01:01:55,800 --> 01:01:58,800
Reserve requirements being 10%.

1322
01:01:58,800 --> 01:01:59,800
And that's only $12.

1323
01:01:59,800 --> 01:02:01,800
And we've got it in cash.

1324
01:02:01,800 --> 01:02:05,800
And we're satisfying our Basel III capital requirements.

1325
01:02:05,800 --> 01:02:08,800
So, everything is fine.

1326
01:02:08,800 --> 01:02:09,800
Everything is fine.

1327
01:02:09,800 --> 01:02:10,800
And we're in business.

1328
01:02:10,800 --> 01:02:12,800
And we're not done yet.

1329
01:02:12,800 --> 01:02:15,800
Because our risk-weighted assets, our

1330
01:02:15,800 --> 01:02:18,800
$7 is the capital requirement.

1331
01:02:18,800 --> 01:02:21,800
And we've got $20 in common equity.

1332
01:02:21,800 --> 01:02:23,800
Okay.

1333
01:02:23,800 --> 01:02:24,800
So, everything is great.

1334
01:02:24,800 --> 01:02:25,800
This is fine.

1335
01:02:25,800 --> 01:02:26,800
But now, let's go on.

1336
01:02:26,800 --> 01:02:27,800
Now, there's a crisis.

1337
01:02:27,800 --> 01:02:28,800
All right?

1338
01:02:28,800 --> 01:02:30,800
Business gets bad.

1339
01:02:30,800 --> 01:02:33,800
So, the next thing that happens

1340
01:02:33,800 --> 01:02:36,800
is 20% of my corporate loans default.

1341
01:02:36,800 --> 01:02:38,800
Okay?

1342
01:02:38,800 --> 01:02:41,800
And so, we then, you know, it becomes clear.

1343
01:02:41,800 --> 01:02:43,800
We have another board meeting.

1344
01:02:43,800 --> 01:02:45,800
And someone says, I have bad news.

1345
01:02:45,800 --> 01:02:47,800
We made $100 in corporate loans.

1346
01:02:47,800 --> 01:02:49,300
But these guys, $20 is

1347
01:02:49,300 --> 01:02:50,800
never going to get paid back.

1348
01:02:50,800 --> 01:02:52,800
The borrower is out of business.

1349
01:02:52,800 --> 01:02:54,800
So, I suggest we do

1350
01:02:54,800 --> 01:02:56,800
a write-off of our corporate loans.

1351
01:02:56,800 --> 01:02:59,800
And let's reduce them to $80.

1352
01:02:59,800 --> 01:03:01,800
Okay?

1353
01:03:01,800 --> 01:03:03,800
So, what happens?

1354
01:03:03,800 --> 01:03:07,800
Now, assets have to equal liabilities.

1355
01:03:07,800 --> 01:03:10,800
$100 is our assets now, not $120.

1356
01:03:10,800 --> 01:03:11,800
Right?

1357
01:03:11,800 --> 01:03:13,800
Because we just lost $20.

1358
01:03:13,800 --> 01:03:16,300
Our liabilities can't be $120 because

1359
01:03:16,300 --> 01:03:18,800
they have to equal our assets.

1360
01:03:18,800 --> 01:03:19,800
What gives?

1361
01:03:19,800 --> 01:03:21,800
Well, it's the shareholders that give.

1362
01:03:21,800 --> 01:03:25,800
So, we mark down common equity to zero.

1363
01:03:25,800 --> 01:03:30,800
And now, assets and liabilities match.

1364
01:03:30,800 --> 01:03:33,800
So, now, let's look at our requirements.

1365
01:03:33,800 --> 01:03:36,800
What about our reserve requirements?

1366
01:03:36,800 --> 01:03:38,800
Reserve requirements are fine.

1367
01:03:38,800 --> 01:03:40,800
We're holding excess reserves.

1368
01:03:40,800 --> 01:03:43,800
You know, we only have to hold $10.

1369
01:03:43,800 --> 01:03:45,800
And we've got $20 in cash.

1370
01:03:45,800 --> 01:03:48,800
But we're no longer satisfying our capital requirements.

1371
01:03:48,800 --> 01:03:50,800
So, our bank regulator is going to show us,

1372
01:03:50,800 --> 01:03:54,300
our bank regulator is going to shut us

1373
01:03:54,300 --> 01:03:57,800
down unless we do something to raise capital.

1374
01:03:57,800 --> 01:03:59,800
So, how do we raise capital?

1375
01:03:59,800 --> 01:04:00,800
That's the next step.

1376
01:04:00,800 --> 01:04:04,300
Well, one thing we could do is

1377
01:04:04,300 --> 01:04:07,800
we could sell some of our corporate loans.

1378
01:04:07,800 --> 01:04:09,800
Right?

1379
01:04:09,800 --> 01:04:12,800
We could find a buyer for our corporate loans.

1380
01:04:12,800 --> 01:04:15,800
And we could sell, say, $20 worth of them.

1381
01:04:15,800 --> 01:04:17,800
Bring this down to $60.

1382
01:04:17,800 --> 01:04:18,800
And this would go up to $40.

1383
01:04:18,800 --> 01:04:19,800
All right?

1384
01:04:19,800 --> 01:04:22,800
So, our risk-weighted assets would now –

1385
01:04:22,800 --> 01:04:23,800
well, then, let me see.

1386
01:04:23,800 --> 01:04:24,800
No, that wouldn't do it.

1387
01:04:24,800 --> 01:04:25,800
I'm sorry.

1388
01:04:25,800 --> 01:04:26,800
That wouldn't do it, would it?

1389
01:04:26,800 --> 01:04:27,800
We'd still have – we

1390
01:04:27,800 --> 01:04:28,800
still don't have – I'm sorry.

1391
01:04:28,800 --> 01:04:29,800
I misspoke.

1392
01:04:29,800 --> 01:04:32,800
We still don't have any common equity.

1393
01:04:32,800 --> 01:04:35,800
In this case, if we don't have any common equity,

1394
01:04:35,800 --> 01:04:38,800
we can't get out of this by selling our loans.

1395
01:04:38,800 --> 01:04:41,800
We could have if it didn't reduce it to zero.

1396
01:04:41,800 --> 01:04:44,800
If the common equity went down to $10, then I could

1397
01:04:44,800 --> 01:04:47,800
sell some of my corporate loans to get out of this mess.

1398
01:04:47,800 --> 01:04:48,800
Right?

1399
01:04:48,800 --> 01:04:49,800
But I made it zero.

1400
01:04:49,800 --> 01:04:51,800
So, the only way out that I

1401
01:04:51,800 --> 01:04:53,800
can do is to issue more shares.

1402
01:04:53,800 --> 01:04:56,800
So, I've got to go to my friends again and

1403
01:04:56,800 --> 01:04:59,800
say, well, we started this bank, but we goofed up.

1404
01:04:59,800 --> 01:05:01,800
We made bad loans.

1405
01:05:01,800 --> 01:05:04,800
And so, we've exhausted our common equity.

1406
01:05:04,800 --> 01:05:10,800
I've got to raise more capital now.

1407
01:05:10,800 --> 01:05:12,800
And so, what you could do

1408
01:05:12,800 --> 01:05:14,800
is get more friends to come in.

1409
01:05:14,800 --> 01:05:16,300
Now, they might not want to do it,

1410
01:05:16,300 --> 01:05:17,800
because the previous friends just got wiped out.

1411
01:05:17,800 --> 01:05:19,800
They lost everything, right?

1412
01:05:19,800 --> 01:05:22,300
But you're coming in as new

1413
01:05:22,300 --> 01:05:24,800
shareholders on top of the old.

1414
01:05:24,800 --> 01:05:27,800
And you could then go back to where

1415
01:05:27,800 --> 01:05:30,800
you were before by just issuing more shares.

1416
01:05:30,800 --> 01:05:32,800
So, this is the system.

1417
01:05:32,800 --> 01:05:35,800
I think I've pretty much summarized it.

1418
01:05:35,800 --> 01:05:37,800
It's simple.

1419
01:05:37,800 --> 01:05:41,800
Now, the issue is, however, that what

1420
01:05:41,800 --> 01:05:45,800
motivated Basel III was that the –

1421
01:05:47,800 --> 01:05:50,800
this system of requiring banks to hold

1422
01:05:50,800 --> 01:05:53,800
capital is supposed to stabilize the system.

1423
01:05:53,800 --> 01:05:57,300
If they've got enough common equity, the bank won't go

1424
01:05:57,300 --> 01:06:00,800
bankrupt, even if they lose some of their corporate loans.

1425
01:06:00,800 --> 01:06:03,300
It would be a big

1426
01:06:03,300 --> 01:06:05,800
disaster to drive them to insolvency.

1427
01:06:05,800 --> 01:06:10,800
But the problem is that the system they set up

1428
01:06:10,800 --> 01:06:15,800
has banks responding to a crisis by selling corporate loans,

1429
01:06:15,800 --> 01:06:19,800
or selling – issuing new shares.

1430
01:06:19,800 --> 01:06:21,800
The problem is, both of those

1431
01:06:21,800 --> 01:06:23,800
are hard to do in a crisis.

1432
01:06:23,800 --> 01:06:25,800
In a crisis, you know, and everything

1433
01:06:25,800 --> 01:06:27,800
is falling apart, you go out saying,

1434
01:06:27,800 --> 01:06:29,800
I want to issue new shares

1435
01:06:29,800 --> 01:06:31,800
in my bank that just lost everything.

1436
01:06:31,800 --> 01:06:33,800
Investors are going to say, you've got to be kidding.

1437
01:06:33,800 --> 01:06:35,800
I'm not going to invest in you right now.

1438
01:06:35,800 --> 01:06:38,800
So, you can't raise new equity.

1439
01:06:38,800 --> 01:06:40,800
Okay, what about selling loans?

1440
01:06:40,800 --> 01:06:42,300
Well, the problem is, in a

1441
01:06:42,300 --> 01:06:43,800
big national – or international crisis,

1442
01:06:43,800 --> 01:06:44,800
every bank on the planet –

1443
01:06:44,800 --> 01:06:46,800
every bank on the planet is trying

1444
01:06:46,800 --> 01:06:48,800
to sell its loans at the same time.

1445
01:06:48,800 --> 01:06:51,800
So, it creates a collapse in the system.

1446
01:06:51,800 --> 01:06:53,800
And this is what happened in the financial crisis.

1447
01:06:53,800 --> 01:06:56,800
This story repeated a million times,

1448
01:06:56,800 --> 01:06:59,300
was banks were trying to raise capital,

1449
01:06:59,300 --> 01:07:01,800
and they were trying to sell assets,

1450
01:07:01,800 --> 01:07:04,800
either by issuing – they were trying to raise

1451
01:07:04,800 --> 01:07:07,800
capital either by issuing shares or by selling assets.

1452
01:07:07,800 --> 01:07:09,300
And everyone doing it at

1453
01:07:09,300 --> 01:07:10,800
the same time created a crunch.

1454
01:07:10,800 --> 01:07:12,800
And the whole system would have collapsed

1455
01:07:12,800 --> 01:07:14,800
if it weren't for the central banks.

1456
01:07:14,800 --> 01:07:18,800
And the central banks of the

1457
01:07:18,800 --> 01:07:22,800
world responded quickly by loans to companies,

1458
01:07:22,800 --> 01:07:25,800
to banks and other companies.

1459
01:07:25,800 --> 01:07:27,800
So, the lender of last resort

1460
01:07:27,800 --> 01:07:29,800
saved the whole system from collapsing.

1461
01:07:29,800 --> 01:07:33,800
That's the story of the financial crisis.

1462
01:07:33,800 --> 01:07:36,300
The remaining story is the

1463
01:07:36,300 --> 01:07:38,800
Basel III people in Switzerland said,

1464
01:07:38,800 --> 01:07:41,800
let's analyze how we got into this situation.

1465
01:07:41,800 --> 01:07:42,800
How did it get so bad?

1466
01:07:42,800 --> 01:07:44,800
And they thought, you know, it's a big problem.

1467
01:07:44,800 --> 01:07:49,300
And it was kind of a funny system, because

1468
01:07:49,300 --> 01:07:53,800
we're requiring banks to raise capital at the worst time.

1469
01:07:53,800 --> 01:07:56,800
And that can't be the right system.

1470
01:07:56,800 --> 01:07:58,300
And they looked around and tried

1471
01:07:58,300 --> 01:07:59,800
to decide what to do better.

1472
01:07:59,800 --> 01:08:01,800
Most countries of the world

1473
01:08:01,800 --> 01:08:03,800
were following this kind of system.

1474
01:08:03,800 --> 01:08:06,800
There was some – some people were impressed

1475
01:08:06,800 --> 01:08:09,800
by the government of Spain having a better system.

1476
01:08:09,800 --> 01:08:12,800
But the Spanish banking system collapsed anyway.

1477
01:08:12,800 --> 01:08:16,800
So, it didn't solve the problem.

1478
01:08:16,800 --> 01:08:22,300
So, Basel III came up with a

1479
01:08:22,300 --> 01:08:27,800
solution, which was to allow the central regulators

1480
01:08:27,800 --> 01:08:39,800
to add another buffer of 2.5%.

1481
01:08:39,800 --> 01:08:41,800
If they think there's a bubble going on.

1482
01:08:42,800 --> 01:08:45,800
They would do this before the crisis.

1483
01:08:45,800 --> 01:08:52,800
And that raises the common equity requirement to 9.5%.

1484
01:08:52,800 --> 01:08:54,300
So, the idea is, this

1485
01:08:54,300 --> 01:08:55,800
is going to be a problem.

1486
01:08:55,800 --> 01:08:58,800
Raising capital at a time of difficulty

1487
01:08:58,800 --> 01:09:01,800
is always going to be a problem.

1488
01:09:01,800 --> 01:09:04,300
And we can always rely again on

1489
01:09:04,300 --> 01:09:06,800
our central banks, but maybe we can't.

1490
01:09:06,800 --> 01:09:08,800
Maybe we shouldn't.

1491
01:09:08,800 --> 01:09:09,800
And so, the idea is,

1492
01:09:09,800 --> 01:09:10,800
let's take bank regulators in mind.

1493
01:09:10,800 --> 01:09:11,800
Let's take them in mind.

1494
01:09:11,800 --> 01:09:14,800
Let's take bank regulators and make it

1495
01:09:14,800 --> 01:09:17,800
their obligation to raise capital requirements in advance,

1496
01:09:17,800 --> 01:09:19,800
when they see a bubble coming.

1497
01:09:19,800 --> 01:09:23,800
Now, another thing that happened in the

1498
01:09:23,800 --> 01:09:27,800
United States is the Dodd-Frank Act of 2010.

1499
01:09:27,800 --> 01:09:32,800
Because of intense public reaction to all

1500
01:09:32,800 --> 01:09:37,800
the bailouts, said that the Federal Reserve

1501
01:09:37,800 --> 01:09:38,800
can no longer use discretion in

1502
01:09:38,800 --> 01:09:39,800
deciding who's going to be in charge.

1503
01:09:39,800 --> 01:09:43,800
They can operate a discount window, but

1504
01:09:43,800 --> 01:09:47,800
it has to be completely fair and even.

1505
01:09:47,800 --> 01:09:50,800
They can't decide, we're going to bail out Bear

1506
01:09:50,800 --> 01:09:53,800
Stearns, and we're going to let Lehman Brothers fail.

1507
01:09:53,800 --> 01:09:57,800
All they can do is operate a consistent discount window.

1508
01:09:57,800 --> 01:10:01,300
So, the Dodd-Frank Act constrained the

1509
01:10:01,300 --> 01:10:04,800
central bank in the United States

1510
01:10:04,800 --> 01:10:06,300
from exercising the kind of judgment

1511
01:10:06,300 --> 01:10:07,800
that saved us from the crisis.

1512
01:10:07,800 --> 01:10:09,800
So, we're going to have to do something about it.

1513
01:10:09,800 --> 01:10:12,800
And we're going to have to rely

1514
01:10:12,800 --> 01:10:15,800
on some different things, like better capital standards,

1515
01:10:15,800 --> 01:10:21,800
like Basel III, to fix the situation.

1516
01:10:21,800 --> 01:10:23,300
Whether we're there or not is

1517
01:10:23,300 --> 01:10:24,800
going to be a big question.

1518
01:10:24,800 --> 01:10:28,300
This is a complicated system, and we put a

1519
01:10:28,300 --> 01:10:31,800
lot of the best minds into trying to figure out

1520
01:10:31,800 --> 01:10:34,300
how to prevent the kind of

1521
01:10:34,300 --> 01:10:36,800
instability that we're seeing in this example,

1522
01:10:36,800 --> 01:10:39,800
where everyone is short on capital at the same time,

1523
01:10:39,800 --> 01:10:42,300
everyone is selling loans at the

1524
01:10:42,300 --> 01:10:44,800
same time, and the whole system collapses.

1525
01:10:44,800 --> 01:10:47,300
We've come up with different solutions,

1526
01:10:47,300 --> 01:10:49,800
but they have to be implemented yet,

1527
01:10:49,800 --> 01:10:51,800
and there are problems of implementation.

1528
01:10:51,800 --> 01:10:55,800
The role of central banks and

1529
01:10:55,800 --> 01:10:59,800
of regulatory authorities are evolving and changing,

1530
01:10:59,800 --> 01:11:03,800
and it'll be a period of many years

1531
01:11:03,800 --> 01:11:07,800
before we know where this system is actually going.

1532
01:11:07,800 --> 01:11:09,800
All right, I will stop here.

1533
01:11:09,800 --> 01:11:13,800
Next lecture is on investment banking,

1534
01:11:13,800 --> 01:11:19,800
and we have a former Econ 252 student, Jan Fugner,

1535
01:11:19,800 --> 01:11:22,800
who is back after nine years,

1536
